      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment carefully.\n\nThis is page 1 of 5 (certificate cover) plus page 2 and page 3 of an OIML Certificate R 137/2012-B-NL1-18.04.\n\nDocument-level:\n- number: \"R 137/2012-B-NL1-18.04\"\n- scheme: \"B\"\n- project_number: \"1901860\"\n- page_total: 5\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- date_issued: \"2018-09-24\" (24 September 2018)\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\" (NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1)\n\nIssuing authority:\n- name: \"NMi Certin B.V.\"\n- address_lines: [\"Hugo de Grootplein 1\", \"3314 EG Dordrecht\", \"The Netherlands\"]\n- person_responsible: \"C. Oosterman\"\n- person_title: \"Head Certification Board\"\n- phone: \"+31 78 6332332\"\n- email: \"certin@nmi.nl\"\n- website: \"www.nmi.nl\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\nApplicants:\n- name: \"Flow Meter Group B.V.\"\n- address_lines: [\"Innovatieweg 32\", \"7007 CD Doetinchem\", \"The Netherlands\"]\n\nManufacturers: same — labeled \"Applicant and Manufacturer\". So manufacturers[]: same entry.\n\nCertified type:\n- category: \"Gas meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"FMR\", \"FMR-Dual\"]\n- description: \"A rotary piston gas meter\"\n- module_designation: \"Not applicable\" — actually Table 1 says Electromagnetic class \"Not applicable (the meter has no electronics)\". Module designation isn't stated; leave it out or null. Better to omit.\n\nRecommendation:\n- id: \"R137\" (canonical). The recommendation is \"R 137-1 (2012)\". Hmm — R 137-1. Canonical id per rules \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\". So id: \"R137-1\"? The recommendation is R 137-1 with edition 2012. Let me set id: \"R137-1\", edition: 2012. Actually edition should be int: 2012. Amendment: none.\n- accuracy_classes: from certificate: \"1,0 and/or 1,5\" → [1.0, 1.5]. Also table has accuracy class per model. The certificate-level accuracy_classes: [\"1,0\", \"1,5\"] as numbers 1.0 and 1.5.\n\nTest reports:\n- No. NMi-14200712-02 dated 30 January 2015, 36 pages — role: type_evaluation_report (these are reports; the schema says role: type_evaluation_report | test_report | documentation_file | evaluation_report). These are probably Type Evaluation Reports. I'll assign \"type_evaluation_report\" for the first? Hard to distinguish. All three listed as \"results of tests and examinations provided in the associated report(s)\". I'll mark them all as \"type_evaluation_report\" — hmm, but maybe \"test_report\". Given ambiguity, use \"type_evaluation_report\" for the first (largest, contains Type Evaluation) and \"test_report\" for the others? Actually rule says don't fabricate. The role is uncertain; the safest: role \"test_report\"? The text: \"This Certificate relates ... identified in the OIML Type Evaluation Report ... The conformity was established by the results of tests and examinations provided in the associated report(s)\". These associated reports are the type evaluation reports. I'll set role: \"type_evaluation_report\" for all three.\n\nDocumentation folder no. T10372-8 — role: documentation_file. id: \"T10372-8\".\n\nTable 1 General characteristics (type_level):\n- destined_for_measurement_of: \"Gas volume\" (string value)\n- mechanical_class: \"M1\"\n- electromagnetic_class: \"Not applicable (the meter has no electronics)\" — string value\n- ambient_temperature_range: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit \"°C\"\n- gas_temperature_range: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit \"°C\"\n- orientation: \"Horizontal / Vertical up / Vertical down (all orientations)\"\n- flow_direction: \"Uni-directional (indicated with arrow)\"\n\nThese are type_level — values can be strings when non-numeric. The structured value shape: value can be string. For temperature: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit_symbol \"°C\", unit_id maybe \"u:degree_Celsius\" — I'm fairly certain that's the unitsdb ID given in the example. Rule 3 says \"Leave unit_id: null unless you are certain of the unitsdb ID.\" The example in the schema shows \"u:degree_Celsius\", so I can use that for °C.\n\nTable 2 — family of instruments. This is a big matrix table. Columns:\n- Volume V [dm³] (cyclic volume)\n- G-value\n- Qmax [m³/h]\n- minimum Qmin [m³/h]\n- Qt [m³/h]\n- maximum pmax [bar]\n- Diameter D [mm]\n- Accuracy class\n\nThe table has an \"FMR\" header spanning 8 columns. Presumably FMR-Dual has same characteristics (title says \"family of instruments (FMR and FMR-Dual)\"). The table only shows FMR header; maybe FMR-Dual table follows on page 4/5 (not in this fragment). So this table is for FMR.\n\nNow the rows. Let me enumerate them. Volume is a rowspan cell (merged). Decimal commas: 0,25 → 0.25, etc.\n\nRows (volume, G, Qmax, Qmin, Qt, pmax, Diameter, Accuracy class):\n1. 0.25, G6, 10, 0.25, 0.5, 101, Threaded, 1.0\n2. 0.25, G10, 16, 0.25, 0.8, 101, Threaded, 1.0\n3. 0.25, G16, 25, 0.25, 1.25, 101, Threaded, 1.0\n4. 0.25, G25, 40, 0.25, 2, 101, Threaded, 1.0 or 1.5\n5. 0.39, G10, 16, 0.25, 0.8, 101, \"40 or 50\", 1.0\n6. 0.39, G16, 25, 0.25, 1.25, 101, \"40 or 50\", 1.0\n7. 0.39, G25, 40, 0.25, 2, 101, \"40 or 50\", 1.0 or 1.5\n8. 0.39, G40, 65, 0.25, 3.2, 101, \"40 or 50\", 1.0 or 1.5\n9. 0.61, G16, 25, 0.25, 1.25, 101, \"40 or 50\", 1.0\n10. 0.61, G25, 40, 0.25, 2, 101, \"40 or 50\", 1.0 or 1.5\n11. 0.61, G40, 65, 0.25, 3.2, 101, \"40 or 50\", 1.0 or 1.5\n12. 0.61, G65, 100, 0.25, 5, 101, \"40 or 50\", 1.0 or 1.5\n13. 0.73, G16, 25, 0.2, 1.25, 101, \"40 or 50\", 1.0\n14. 0.73, G25, 40, 0.2, 2, 101, \"40 or 50\", 1.0 or 1.5\n15. 0.73, G40, 65, 0.2, 3.2, 101, \"40 or 50\", 1.0 or 1.5\n16. 0.73, G65, 100, 0.2, 5, 101, \"40 or 50\", 1.0 or 1.5\n17. (blank volume, continues 0.73), G100, 160, 0.4, 8, 12, \"50 or 80\", 1.0 or 1.5\n18. 0.85, G25, 40, 1, 2, 101, \"50 or 80\", 1.0\n19. 0.85, G40, 65, 1, 3.2, 101, \"50 or 80\", 1.0\n20. 0.85, G65, 100, 1, 5, 101, \"50 or 80\", 1.0\n21. (blank → 0.85), G100, 160, 1, 8, 12, \"50 or 80\", 1.0 or 1.5\n22. 0.99, G25, 40, 1, 2, 101, \"50 or 80\", 1.0\n23. 0.99, G40, 65, 1, 3.2, 101, \"50 or 80\", 1.0\n24. 0.99, G65, 100, 1, 5, 101, \"50 or 80\", 1.0\n25. 0.99, G100, 160, 1, 8, 101, \"50 or 80\", 1.0 or 1.5\n26. 1.16, G40, 65, 0.4, 3.2, 101, \"50 or 80\", 1.0 or 1.5\n27. 1.16, G65, 100, 0.4, 5, 101, \"50 or 80\", 1.0 or 1.5\n28. 1.16, G100, 160, 0.4, 8, 101, \"50 or 80\", 1.0 or 1.5\n29. (blank → 1.16), G160, 250, 0.65, 12.5, 12, \"50 or 80\", 1.0 or 1.5\n30. 1.45, G65, 100, 0.6, 5, 101, \"80 or 100\", 1.0 or 1.5\n31. 1.45, G100, 160, 0.6, 8, 101, \"80 or 100\", 1.0 or 1.5\n32. 1.45, G160, 250, 0.6, 12.5, 101, \"80 or 100\", 1.0 or 1.5\n33. 1.81, G65, 100, 0.6, 5, 101, \"80 or 100\", 1.0 or 1.5\n34. 1.81, G100, 160, 0.6, 8, 101, \"80 or 100\", 1.0 or 1.5\n35. 1.81, G160, 250, 0.6, 12.5, 101, \"80 or 100\", 1.0 or 1.5\n36. (blank → 1.81), G250, 400, 1, 20, 12, \"80 or 100\", 1.0 or 1.5\n37. 1.98, G100, 160, 1, 8, 101, \"80 or 100\", 1.0 or 1.5\n38. 1.98, G160, 250, 1, 12.5, 101, \"80 or 100\", 1.0 or 1.5\n39. (blank → 1.98), G250, 400, 2.5, 20, 12, \"80 or 100\", 1.0 or 1.5\n40. 3.17, G160, 250, 1.6, 12.5, 101, \"80 or 100\", 1.0 or 1.5\n41. 3.17, G250, 400, 1.6, 20, 101, \"80 or 100\", 1.0 or 1.5\n42. (blank → 3.17), G400, 650, 2.5, 32, 12, \"80 or 100\", 1.0 or 1.5\n43. 3.96, G250, 400, 2.5, 20, 101, \"100 or 150\", 1.0 or 1.5\n44. 3.96, G400, 650, 4, 32, 101, \"100 or 150\", 1.0 or 1.5\n45. (blank → 3.96), G650, 1000, 4, 32, 12, \"150 or 200\", 1.0 or 1.5\n46. 5.15, G250, 400, 2.5, 20, 101, \"100 or 150\", 1.0 or 1.5\n47. 5.15, G400, 650, 2.5, 32, 101, \"100 or 150\", 1.0 or 1.5\n48. (blank → 5.15), G650, 1000, 6.25, 50, 12, \"100 or 150\", 1.0 or 1.5\n\nThat's 48 rows. Note interesting: rows 17, 21, 29, 36, 39, 42, 45, 48 — the volume cell is blank, meaning they continue the previous volume group (rowspan continuation). Wait — actually looking at the structure: e.g. \"0,73\" rowspan=4 covers G16, G25, G40, G65, then the G100 row has an empty volume cell. Hmm, that's odd — rowspan 4 covers 4 rows, and then the 5th row has an empty td for volume. Does the G100 belong to the 0.73 group or is it a separate group with unspecified volume? Similarly \"0,85\" rowspan=3 covers G25, G40, G65, then G100 row has empty volume cell.\n\nHmm. Looking at row 16: 0.73 group rowspan=4 → G16, G25, G40, G65. Then row 17 has `<td></td>` empty volume, G100, 160, 0.4, 8, 12, 50 or 80.\n\nThen \"0,85\" rowspan=3 → G25, G40, G65 (rows 18-20). Then row 21: empty volume, G100.\n\nThen \"0,99\" rowspan=4 → G25, G40, G65, G100 (rows 22-25). No empty rows there.\n\nSo for the G100 rows with empty volume cells: they likely belong to the same physical meter size as the preceding group but with pmax=12 (not 101). E.g., 0.73 volume with G100 at pmax 12 bar. Or possibly the empty cell means \"same as above\" (continuation of the volume value). Given the rowspan is already exhausted (0.73 rowspan=4 covers exactly 4 rows), the 5th row's empty cell would technically be outside the rowspan... In HTML rendering, if a rowspan cell has ended, an empty td would render as an empty cell. But semantically it likely means \"same volume as above\" — this is common in such tables where the merged cell covers visually but OCR split it.\n\nActually wait — consider the pattern: G100 at higher flows (160 m³/h) can't handle 101 bar (that's a very high pressure — 101 bar is typical max for these rotary meters). The G100 at 0.73-0.99 dm³ would need a bigger volume. Hmm. Let me think about the real Flow Meter Group FMR meter. The pattern suggests: each row = a specific meter model. Volume blank rows: probably the volume is genuinely the same as the previous row (the rowspan was set to exclude the last row erroneously, or the G100 has same volume but different pressure rating).\n\nFor G650 rows: \"5,15\" rowspan=2 covers G250, G400; then G650 empty. Then G650 at 1000 m³/h, Qmin 4, Qt 32, pmax 12, diameter 150 or 200. Compare with row 45 (3.96 blank): G650, 1000, 4, 32, 12, 150 or 200. Same values. Hmm, interesting — row 45 belongs to 3.96 group (blank cell after rowspan=2 of 3,96 covering G250, G400).\n\nActually, I think the interpretation is: the empty cell means the volume continues from above (same volume value). The rowspan count was 4 for 0.73 but there are 5 rows in that visual group; the OCR/HTML may have miscounted, or the original PDF has the G100 in a visually separate block. Either way, safest interpretation for extraction: empty volume = same as previous volume (continuation). I'll propagate the previous volume value for blank cells and note in description.\n\nHmm, but is that fabrication? The rows are visually grouped under a single volume; blank typically means \"ditto\". I'll propagate. Alternatively, use null. Let me think about what's more useful and accurate. In these NMi tables, the volume column with rowspan covers N rows; when rowspan=N but N+1 rows appear, the last row's blank cell means continuation. Given the 0.73 group: G16, G25, G40, G65 at pmax 101, then G100 at pmax 12 — the volume 0.73 applies. Actually hmm, does a 0.73 dm³ G100 meter exist? G100 at Qmax=160 m³/h... cyclic volume 0.73 dm³ would require very high rotation speed. Typically G100 needs ~1 dm³+. For the 0.99 group, G100 is included with pmax=101. For 0.73, G100 with pmax=12 (low pressure) — that seems odd physically but could be for low-pressure applications where faster rotation is acceptable.\n\nAlternatively the blank could mean the G100 belongs to the NEXT group? No — the next group header comes after it (0.85 rowspan=3 starts right after the blank G100 row). So blank = continuation of previous group's volume is the only sensible reading.\n\nHmm wait, actually re-reading: maybe the structure is that the rowspan cells cover exactly the rows listed, and the blank-volume rows are separate variants where volume is not applicable/different. But G-values are unique identifiers; each row = one meter variant. The blank is a ditto. I'll propagate.\n\nAlso note pmax=12 for these G100/G160/G250/G400/G650 rows — 12 bar (low pressure) vs 101 bar (high pressure). That's consistent with physical reality: larger G meters with same cyclic volume... wait no. Actually for high pressure (101 bar), meters need smaller G ratings for the same volume... The pmax=12 rows have the same volume as the group. E.g., 0.73 dm³, G100, pmax 12. And 0.99 dm³, G100, pmax 101. So a 0.73 dm³ meter can be G100 at max 12 bar but the 0.99 can be G100 at 101 bar. Plausible: at higher pressure, gas density higher, torque higher.\n\nOK, decision: propagate blank volume cells with previous volume, and include in matrix rows.\n\nModel family: This cert covers a family of instruments (FMR and FMR-Dual). Each table row is a model variant identified by G-value (and volume/pmax). The schema example for R137 (this exact recommendation!) shows models identified by size \"G1.6\", \"G2.5\". Here there are 48 rows. Model ID should probably combine volume + G-value + pmax where ambiguous. But G-values repeat (e.g., G25 appears at volumes 0.25, 0.39, 0.61, 0.73, 0.85, 0.99). Hmm.\n\nThe instruction example uses \"G1.6\", \"G2.5\" as model IDs. Here, G-values alone don't uniquely identify rows. I could use model_id like \"G25-V0.25\", \"G25-V0.39\"... or \"0.25/G25\". Given complexity, I think a good approach: model_id = \"V0.25-G6\" style, or just list in matrix_tables with rows keyed by (volume, G-value, pmax). \n\nActually, wait — do G-value and volume uniquely identify? Check duplicates: \n- G10 at 0.25? No — G10 appears at 0.25? Row 2: 0.25 G10; row 5: 0.39 G10. Unique.\n- G16: 0.25, 0.39, 0.61, 0.73 — unique per volume.\n- G25: 0.25, 0.39, 0.61, 0.73, 0.85, 0.99 — unique.\n- G40: 0.39, 0.61, 0.73, 0.85, 0.99, 1.16 — unique.\n- G65: 0.61, 0.73, 0.85, 0.99, 1.16, 1.45, 1.81 — unique.\n- G100: 0.73(blank), 0.85(blank), 0.99, 1.16, 1.45, 1.81, 1.98 — 0.73 and 0.85 unique; G100 appears twice at 0.99? No — row 25 only. OK unique.\n- G160: 1.16(blank), 1.45, 1.81, 1.98, 3.17 — unique.\n- G250: 1.81(blank), 1.98(blank), 3.17, 3.96, 5.15 — unique.\n- G400: 3.17(blank), 3.96, 5.15 — unique.\n- G650: 3.96(blank), 5.15(blank) — hmm! Row 45: volume 3.96 (blank), G650, 1000, 4, 32, 12, 150 or 200. Row 48: volume 5.15 (blank), G650, 1000, 6.25, 50, 12, 100 or 150. Different Qmin/Qt/diameter but same G650. So (volume, G) unique: 3.96/G650 vs 5.15/G650. Good.\n\nSo volume+G uniquely identifies each row. Model ID: \"V0.25-G6\" etc. Or maybe keep simpler: since the schema example just uses G-value... but here G alone is ambiguous. I'll use format \"V0.25/G6\" — hmm, or with pmax? Row 17: 0.73/G100 pmax12 vs 0.99/G100 pmax101 — different volumes anyway. I'll use volume+G: \"V0.25-G6\".\n\nHmm, actually maybe cleaner: model_id = \"G6 (0.25 dm³)\". Let me just pick \"V0.25-G6\" style. Or follow example: they used \"G1.6\" because that was unique. Here I'll do \"0.25-G6\"? I'll go with \"V0.25-G6\" — descriptive and unambiguous.\n\nActually, let me reconsider — maybe simpler to keep model_id as \"G6\" etc. but that loses uniqueness. I'll do \"V0.25-G6\".\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety: the FMR-Dual. The table header says FMR only (colspan=8 \"FMR\"). The caption says \"family of instruments (FMR and FMR-Dual)\". The FMR-Dual characteristics table is probably on pages 4-5 (not in this fragment). So I should label these models as FMR models. Maybe model_id: \"FMR V0.25-G6\"? Or keep the family note. I'll set family_name: \"FMR and FMR-Dual\" and models with label \"FMR\". Actually the model entries could include \"attributes\": {\"series\": \"FMR\"}. Let me do:\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"FMR and FMR-Dual\" (from table caption \"family of instruments (FMR and FMR-Dual)\")\n- models[]: 48 entries for FMR variants. Each: model_id, label \"FMR\", attributes maybe {}.\n\nHmm, 48 models with attributes might be overkill since the matrix_tables and model_level capture everything. The schema example populated both matrix_tables AND model_family.models AND model_level. The R137 example had only 2 models. With 48, model_level entries for qmax etc. would have 48 values each — that's a lot but it's the \"MOST IMPORTANT\" rule. Let me decide: emit matrix_tables with the full 48-row table, and model_level for the key varying attributes. That could be huge. Let me count: attributes that vary: cyclic_volume (0.25...5.15), qmax (10...1000), qmin, qt, pmax (101 or 12), diameter (Threaded, \"40 or 50\", ...), accuracy_class.\n\nThat's 7 attributes × 48 values = 336 value objects. That's large but doable. Alternatively, I can rely primarily on matrix_tables and include model_level for the main flow characteristics (qmax, qt, qmin) like the example did (example had qmax, qt, qmin in model_level and everything in matrix_tables). The example gave: matrix_tables with full rows, model_level for qmax/qt/qmin only (not cyclic_volume, pressure loss, pmax). I'll follow that pattern: model_level for qmax, qmin, qt (the flow characteristics), and leave cyclic_volume/pmax/diameter/accuracy_class in matrix rows and model attributes.\n\nHmm, but diameter and accuracy class are important. They're in matrix_tables fully. Fine.\n\nLet me reconsider whether to include model_family.models with all 48. The example did include models[] with model_id entries. I'll include all 48 with attributes {\"cyclic_volume\": 0.25, \"pmax\": 101, \"diameter\": \"Threaded\", \"accuracy_class\": ...}? That duplicates matrix. To keep response manageable, I'll include models[] with just model_id and label \"FMR\". Actually, the attributes{} field is optional. I'll keep it light.\n\nNow, decimal conversion: all commas → dots. 0,25 → 0.25; 1,25 → 1.25; 3,2 → 3.2; 12,5 → 12.5; 0,65 → 0.65; 1,6 → 1.6; 6,25 → 6.25.\n\nAccuracy class \"1,0 or 1,5\" → value: \"1.0 or 1.5\"? For matrix rows, I could represent accuracy_class as string \"1.0 or 1.5\" or list [1.0, 1.5]. In matrix rows (objects per row), values are plain numbers/strings. I'll use string \"1.0 or 1.5\" or array [1.0, 1.5]. Arrays are cleaner: \"accuracy_class\": [1.0, 1.5] — but rows[] is \"object per row\" without strict typing; I'll use strings \"1.0\" / \"1.0 or 1.5\"? Hmm. Let me use numbers where single, and a two-element array where alternatives: e.g. \"accuracy_class\": 1.0 or \"accuracy_class\": [1.0, 1.5]. I'll go with that — it's unambiguous. Actually, to be safe and consistent, maybe string \"1.0 or 1.5\" is more faithful. JSON parseability: arrays are fine. I'll use arrays.\n\nDiameter: \"Threaded\", \"40 or 50\", \"50 or 80\", \"80 or 100\", \"100 or 150\", \"150 or 200\" — unit mm. In matrix rows: \"diameter_mm\": \"Threaded\" or [40,50]. I'll use [40, 50] arrays and \"Threaded\" string.\n\npmax: 101 or 12, unit bar.\n\nVolume: unit dm³.\n\nTable columns for matrix_tables:\n- name: \"General characteristics of the family of instruments (FMR)\" — caption says FMR and FMR-Dual, but table header is FMR. I'll name it \"General characteristics of the family of instruments (FMR and FMR-Dual) — FMR table\" and note in description that table is headed FMR; FMR-Dual presumably follows on later pages.\n- columns: [\"cyclic_volume\", \"g_value\", \"qmax\", \"qmin\", \"qt\", \"pmax\", \"diameter\", \"accuracy_class\"]\n- column_units: [\"dm³\", null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"bar\", \"mm\", null]\n\nWait, column header: \"Volume* V [dm³]\" with footnote \"* See remark on next page.\" So there's a footnote marker \"*\" on volume. The footnote text: \"* See remark on next page.\" — record in footnotes[]: marker \"*\", text \"See remark on next page.\" And cyclic_volume values in matrix rows... footnote_markers is on characteristic values, matrix rows are plain objects. I can note in table description/footnotes field. matrix_tables has \"footnotes[]\" — I'll put \"See remark on next page.\" there with marker \"*\".\n\nColumn header says \"minimum Qmin\" — Qmin column. And \"maximum pmax\". \n\nAlso note: header order is Volume, G-value, Qmax, minimum Qmin, Qt, maximum pmax, Diameter, Accuracy class.\n\nNow model_level entries:\n- qmax: unit m³/h, 48 values\n- qmin: unit m³/h, 48 values\n- qt: unit m³/h, 48 values\n\nShould I also do cyclic_volume, pmax, diameter, accuracy_class in model_level? The example only did qmax/qt/qmin. I'll include those three to keep it manageable, plus maybe pmax since it's an important differentiator (101 vs 12)? The example omitted pmax from model_level (it was in matrix only). I'll include qmax, qmin, qt, pmax. Hmm... pmax varies within same model? No — each row has a single pmax. It's fine either way. I'll do qmax, qmin, qt, and also cyclic_volume and pmax for completeness? That's 5 × 48 = 240 value objects. Getting long. Let me do qmax, qmin, qt (following example exactly) — and cyclic_volume, pmax, diameter, accuracy_class remain in matrix rows. Actually, I think adding pmax is valuable. Eh — I'll follow the example: qmax, qt, qmin only. The matrix_table has everything.\n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider model_id format. Given 48 rows, model_id \"V0.25-G6\" is compact. Let me list:\n\n1. V0.25-G6\n2. V0.25-G10\n3. V0.25-G16\n4. V0.25-G25\n5. V0.39-G10\n6. V0.39-G16\n7. V0.39-G25\n8. V0.39-G40\n9. V0.61-G16\n10. V0.61-G25\n11. V0.61-G40\n12. V0.61-G65\n13. V0.73-G16\n14. V0.73-G25\n15. V0.73-G40\n16. V0.73-G65\n17. V0.73-G100\n18. V0.85-G25\n19. V0.85-G40\n20. V0.85-G65\n21. V0.85-G100\n22. V0.99-G25\n23. V0.99-G40\n24. V0.99-G65\n25. V0.99-G100\n26. V1.16-G40\n27. V1.16-G65\n28. V1.16-G100\n29. V1.16-G160\n30. V1.45-G65\n31. V1.45-G100\n32. V1.45-G160\n33. V1.81-G65\n34. V1.81-G100\n35. V1.81-G160\n36. V1.81-G250\n37. V1.98-G100\n38. V1.98-G160\n39. V1.98-G250\n40. V3.17-G160\n41. V3.17-G250\n42. V3.17-G400\n43. V3.96-G250\n44. V3.96-G400\n45. V3.96-G650\n46. V5.15-G250\n47. V5.15-G400\n48. V5.15-G650\n\nNow the values per row for model_level (qmax, qmin, qt):\n\nRow-by-row (qmax, qmin, qt):\n1. G6/0.25: 10, 0.25, 0.5\n2. G10/0.25: 16, 0.25, 0.8\n3. G16/0.25: 25, 0.25, 1.25\n4. G25/0.25: 40, 0.25, 2\n5. G10/0.39: 16, 0.25, 0.8\n6. G16/0.39: 25, 0.25, 1.25\n7. G25/0.39: 40, 0.25, 2\n8. G40/0.39: 65, 0.25, 3.2\n9. G16/0.61: 25, 0.25,