s? Yes — 48 rows as objects. That's the cleanest preservation.\n\nType_level characteristics (Table 1):\n- destined_for_measurement: \"Gas volume\"\n- mechanical_class: \"M1\"\n- electromagnetic_class: \"Not applicable (the meter has no electronics)\"\n- ambient_temperature_range: {min:-25, max:55}, °C\n- gas_temperature_range: {min:-25, max:55}, °C\n- orientation: \"Horizontal / Vertical up / Vertical down (all orientations)\"\n- flow_direction: \"Uni-directional (indicated with arrow)\"\n\nStructured values: for non-numeric, value is string, unit_symbol null, unit_id null. Include unit_symbol/unit_id in all? The shape says value object with value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers. I'll include them consistently; footnote_markers empty arrays or omit? I'll include only when present. To keep JSON smaller, omit empty footnote_markers. The shape shows footnote_markers in the example but I'll include only when non-empty. Hmm, \"Every characteristic value MUST be an object with this shape\". I'll include unit_symbol: null and unit_id: null for string values, and omit footnote_markers when empty. Actually to be safe, include footnote_markers: [] too? That bloats 48×5 entries. model_level values in the schema example are {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5} — plain, no unit objects! Look at rule 4 example: \"values\": [{\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5}, ...]. So model_level values don't need the structured object shape — the unit is at the attribute level. Good, that keeps it compact. Only type_level and config_level use structured objects.\n\nSo type_level: full structured objects. model_level: {model, value}.\n\nRecommendation: id \"R137-1\", edition 2012. Hmm, canonical: the rule says e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\". The recommendation is R 137-1. I'll use \"R137-1\". edition: 2012 (int). accuracy_classes: [1.0, 1.5] from certificate page (\"1,0 and/or 1,5\"). Note the schema field is accuracy_classes[] at recommendation level.\n\nscheme: \"B\".\n\nCertificate: number \"R 137/2012-B-NL1-18.04\". Should I normalize? Keep as-is: \"R 137/2012-B-NL1-18.04\".\n\ndate_issued: \"2018-09-24\" (ISO) — the date appears on page 1: 24 September 2018. Format: I'll use ISO 8601. The schema doesn't specify format; ISO is safest.\n\nTest reports:\n1. id: \"NMi-14200712-02\", date \"2015-01-30\", pages 36, role: type_evaluation_report\n2. id: \"NMi-16200610-01\", date \"2016-09-29\", pages 3, role: type_evaluation_report\n3. id: \"NMi-1901860-01\", date \"2018-04-26\", pages 4, role: type_evaluation_report\n\nHmm, role — \"that includes 36 pages\" etc. These are associated reports. First one is likely the Type Evaluation Report; the later two are additions/amendments (evaluation reports). I'll assign: first \"type_evaluation_report\", others \"test_report\"? Fabricating distinctions is risky. Alternative: all \"type_evaluation_report\". I'll do that, since the cert references \"the OIML Type Evaluation Report\".\n\nDocumentation: \"Documentation folder no. T10372-8\" → test_reports[] entry with role \"documentation_file\", id \"T10372-8\". Date unknown — omit.\n\nRevision history: not present in fragment → omit.\n\nFootnotes: \"* See remark on next page.\" → footnotes[]: {marker: \"*\", text: \"See remark on next page.\"}. Also the cyclic volume column has the \"*\" marker.\n\nModule designation: For a gas meter without electronics, module designation is \"Not applicable\"? The schema module_designation: \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null. Table 1 says electromagnetic class \"Not applicable (the meter has no electronics)\". I could set module_designation: \"Not applicable\". It's reasonable. I'll include \"Not applicable\".\n\nCertified type description: \"A rotary piston gas meter\". category: \"Gas meter\". type_designations: [\"FMR\", \"FMR-Dual\"].\n\nmodel_family.family_name: \"FMR and FMR-Dual\"? The caption: \"General characteristics of the family of instruments (FMR and FMR-Dual)\". The family name is FMR (and FMR-